<h4><span class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Multi-Genre_Focus">Multi-Genre Focus<=
/span><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket"=
>[</span><a title=3D"Edit section: Multi-Genre Focus" class=3D"mw-editsecti=
on-visualeditor" data-tracking-label=3D"edit-section" data-action=3D"edit-s=
ection" href=3D"https://gamedevtycoon.fandom.com/wiki/Game_Development_Base=
d_on_Experience/1.6.11?veaction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13" aria-label=3D"Edit=
 section: Multi-Genre Focus"><svg class=3D"wds-icon wds-icon-tiny" aria-hid=
den=3D"true" focusable=3D"false"><use xlink:href=3D"#wds-icons-pencil-tiny"=
></use></svg></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></h4=
>
<ol><li>For multi-genre combinations, the values are weighted with the firs=
t chosen genre being twice as important as the second one. Refer to the <a =
href=3D"https://gamedevtycoon.fandom.com/wiki/Review_Algorithm#Combination_=
study" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Review Algorithm">Review Algorithm</a=
> for more information on how the following values were calculated.</li></o=
l>
<p>The point of multi-genres is to eliminate a genre's requirements, giving=
 you more flexibility with what features to cram in. For example, Strategy/=
Adventure and Strategy/RPG has only 3 "+" fields and everything else is not=
 bound by rules, while if you'd be making Strategy or RPG, you'd have 6 "+"=
 fields and 1 "-" field to worry about, therefore, multi-genre gives you mo=
re creative freedom when you combine genres properly.
</p><p>The downside to using a multi-genre with less than 2 desired fields =
in a stage is that your game quality will not be increased as much as a mul=
ti-ged fields. <a href=3D"https://gamedevtycoon.fandom.com/wiki/Review_Algo=
rithm#Design_decisions" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Review Algorithm">-s=
ource</a>
</p>
